In that case, you should say that you experience green as a primary but not
orange, rather than that they "are" or "are not" primaries.  (Aside:
E-Prime!  Don't leave home without it!)

I have heard that the 19th century tradition of introspective psychology
collapsed over exactly such questions as "Is green a pure sensation, or is
it a mixture of blue and yellow?", when it turned out that even people
trained within the tradition were unable to reach agreement.  To my visual
sense, green, red, blue, yellow, brown, black, white, grey, purple, and
probably a few others are all "simple" sensations, and the only meaning I
have ever attached to the notion of "primary colour" relates to the mixing
of paints or lights, not subjective experience.  I am in fact quite
perplexed to see it used in the latter way; to me that is like talking
about "primary positions" in a room.  Is the point halfway along a side a
"primary position", or is it a "mixture" of the two adjacent corners? 
(BTW, I second Eric Raymond's booming of Lakoff's "Women, Fire, and
Dangerous Things", and am aware of the Berlin & Kay work summarised there
on "focal colours" and their relation to the physiology of colour
perception; there are far more than a mere three or four focal colours.)

