Ludwig van Beethoven's 9th Symphony is in D-major, not D-minor, contrary to
what Han Huang posted.

"Tomorrow's World" by Joe Jackson (off his "Blaze of Glory" disc) is the most
extropian song I've come across in quite a while.  When I get around to it,
I'll post the lyrics.  After that, I'll compile a tape of all my extropian
songs.  We could all do the same, then exchange them with each other.

I know one of Dr. Bruce Ames' lab assistants (she's the mother of my sister's
ex-fiancee).  His work supports the claim that biogenic chemicals may well be
equally or more harmful than anthropogenic ones, but it doesn't support the
claim that vegetarianism is scientifically bogus.  In fact, Ames himself
reccomends that people eat more fruits and vegetables.  Note that this is
inclusive of fruits and vegetables, rather than exclusive of meat, however.

Eric Raymond's citation of Ames' work to support the claim that vegetarianism
is scientifically bogus seems like a misrepresentation as far as I know.

See the current issue of The American Spectator magazine for an article that
includes a submission Ames made for an environmentalist magazine.  He's one
of the "cornucopian" dissenters from the apocalyptic environmentalist
consensus, like Julian Simon.  Interestingly enough, the apocalyptics loved
him for inventing the Ames test they used to prove that anthropogenics are
carcinogenic and mutagenic; it wasn't until he used it to prove that biogenics
are, too, that they started hating him.  Also, in a Reason interview a while
back, he called himself a "crypto-libertarian," which should amuse Tim May.
He said that Hayek had had some influence upon his thought.

Those experimenting with giving up milk ought to feel the effects within days,
not weeks, but 2 weeks is a good trial period.

In the sex differences thread people are attributing more to ideology and less
to biology than is warranted based upon Kendall's book.  Many of these
behavioral differences are intercultural as well as meeting other criteria
for telling which is the cause.

As for the thread about the relation between the absence of melanin in skin
and drinking milk, low-melanin skin has developed in human societies without
cows or milk, so this theory doesn't explain them.  In fact, "white" people
have evolved in places with lots of sunlight, so the theory that dark skin
is selected for as a protectant against the sun doesn't explain them, either.
While I don't have a reference on this, I read an article about it in a
popular science magazine like Discover a few years ago.  The article concluded
that sexual tastes may have been the selective factor, rather than protection
against sun.

