Date: Tue, 15 Jun 1993 00:57:25 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Methamphetamines over Piracetam

Tim May:
> I second the doubts people are expressing out Dean and Morgenthaler's
> "Mind Drugs." Piracetam, Vincapan, etc., have had no noticeable effect
> on me.

Why should a smart drug have a noticeable effect?  I would much
rather take something that gives me a consistent measurable boost
in thinking (eg larger short-term memory, higher scores on some
important intellectual tasks like working math problems, etc.)
than something with some noticeable (and often annoying) "zing".
For example caffeine can make me drum my fingers like crazy, making
me _look_ alert, but the brain can still feel sleepy/low energy.
I doubt it improves my thinking and there is little evidence to
that effect (and some evidence of the opposite).

Real memory or thinking improvements, unless they were quite
substantial, are probably not something I would consciously notice, 
unless I moved my focus from the task to evaluation of how well I was
performing the task. (In which case, having lost my focus would
likely reduce my performance on the task).

The notion that drugs must have some immdediate sensory feedback
to be "working" seems to be a major handicap to drugs of many kinds --
smart drugs, disease prevention, life extension,  etc.  
Conclusive double-blind scientific studies don't convince people 
emotionally; the "zing" or change in some obvious symptoms 
convinces them.

But if the goal is to alter something that we can't feel,
like intelligence, the prevention of disease, etc. we have to
believe the scientific studies; our body won't tell us what
we need to know.

