Subject: color vision 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Richard Kennaway says:
> I have heard that the 19th century tradition of introspective psychology
> collapsed over exactly such questions as "Is green a pure sensation, or is
> it a mixture of blue and yellow?", when it turned out that even people
> trained within the tradition were unable to reach agreement.  To my visual
> sense, green, red, blue, yellow, brown, black, white, grey, purple, and
> probably a few others are all "simple" sensations, and the only meaning I
> have ever attached to the notion of "primary colour" relates to the mixing
> of paints or lights, not subjective experience.

The question is not meaningless. The reason that primary colors are
used in paint mixing or video is because there are three color
receptors on the back of the eye with particular frequency response
curves. Were those sensors different in number or response curves, our
perception of color would be dramatically different, and objects that
seemed to be particular colors because of mixing of paint or light
would no longer seem to be those colors at all.

Aliens would not see our paintings or videotapes the way we do --
similarly, if one is discussing the question of whether some humans
have different color perception, the question of which colors are
primary is not meaningless.

Perry

